# XSH getrlimit() and setrlimit()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# getrlimit()/setrlimit() are Base (Issue 8) apart from RLIMIT_CPU and the
# SIGXFSZ wording of RLIMIT_FSIZE, which are XSI. Every test that lowers
# a limit does it in a child process that reports through a pipe and
# leaves with _exit(), so the harness is never limited; the parent waits
# with a guard (an expired guard is UNRESOLVED). Lowering never needs
# privilege, so the children only lower; the one test that raises a hard
# limit (EPERM) takes the process unprivileged as far as it can and is
# UNRESOLVED when the raise succeeds (the process then has appropriate
# privileges, which the text leaves to the implementation). Values read
# by getrlimit() that equal RLIM_SAVED_MAX or RLIM_SAVED_CUR are not
# compared. The resource tests pick values from what getrlimit() reports,
# so they hold whatever the starting limits are, and are UNRESOLVED when
# the starting limits leave no room to arrange the state. A setrlimit()
# that "may fail" because usage is already above the new limit (EINVAL) is
# tolerated and ends the test as UNRESOLVED. Not tested: RLIMIT_DATA
# (what counts as the data segment is not specified), RLIMIT_CORE
# (the name and place of a core file are not specified), RLIM_SAVED_MAX and
# RLIM_SAVED_CUR (representability is implementation-defined), the effect
# of exec on limits, the state of the limits after a failed setrlimit()
# (not stated).

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.001
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       getrlimit() returns 0 for each of RLIMIT_CORE, RLIMIT_DATA,
           RLIMIT_FSIZE, RLIMIT_NOFILE, RLIMIT_STACK and RLIMIT_AS, and the
           soft limit is not above the hard limit (RLIM_INFINITY counting as
           above every other value).
test       t/001-get-all.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.002
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       getrlimit(RLIMIT_CPU) returns 0 and the soft limit is not above
           the hard limit.
test       t/002-get-cpu.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.003
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       getrlimit() with a resource value that no resource has (tried:
           -1, INT_MAX, INT_MIN; at least one must be refused) returns -1
           with EINVAL for each that is refused.
note       An implementation may define more resources than the page lists,
           so a candidate that succeeds is skipped; at least one of the
           three must fail.
test       t/003-get-einval.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.004
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       setrlimit() with a resource value that no resource has (tried:
           -1, INT_MAX, INT_MIN, with the limits of RLIMIT_NOFILE as
           read) returns -1 with EINVAL for each that is refused, and at
           least one is refused (run in a child).
test       t/004-set-einval-resource.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.005
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       setrlimit() with rlim_cur larger than rlim_max (RLIMIT_CORE,
           with the hard limit first lowered to a finite value, new soft
           limit hard+1) returns -1 with EINVAL.
test       t/005-set-einval-cur-gt-max.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.006
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       RLIM_INFINITY counts as larger than any other limit: setrlimit()
           with rlim_cur RLIM_INFINITY and a finite rlim_max (RLIMIT_CORE,
           max lowered to a finite value first) returns -1 with EINVAL.
test       t/006-set-einval-inf-cur.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.007
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A process that has lowered a hard limit (RLIMIT_CORE) cannot raise
           it again without appropriate privileges: setrlimit() with the old
           hard limit returns -1 with EPERM. The test is UNRESOLVED when the
           raise succeeds (the process may have appropriate privileges).
note       If the effective user ID is 0 the child first tries to give up
           privilege with setgid() and setuid() to an unlikely user ID.
test       t/007-set-eperm-raise-hard.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.008
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Soft and hard limits can both be lowered in one setrlimit() call
           (soft below the new hard, hard below the old hard): it returns 0
           and getrlimit() then reports exactly the two new values.
test       t/008-set-lower-both.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.009
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The hard limit can be lowered to exactly the soft limit: after
           setrlimit() with both set to the soft value, getrlimit() reports
           that value as both.
test       t/009-set-hard-eq-soft.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.010
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The soft limit can be changed to any value up to the hard limit:
           after lowering the soft limit alone, setrlimit() with the soft
           limit raised back to the hard limit returns 0 and getrlimit()
           reports it, with the hard limit unchanged.
test       t/010-set-soft-up-to-hard.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.011
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       RLIM_INFINITY is a limit value: when the hard limit is
           RLIM_INFINITY, setrlimit() lowering the soft limit and then
           setting it back to RLIM_INFINITY returns 0 and getrlimit()
           reports RLIM_INFINITY. UNRESOLVED when the hard limit of every
           resource tried is finite.
test       t/011-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.012
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/dup.html#ERRORS/EMFILE
class      required
since      2001
says       RLIMIT_NOFILE is one more than the highest descriptor number that
           can be assigned: with the soft limit lowered to N (a little above
           the highest descriptor in use), repeated dup() ends with -1 and
           EMFILE, no descriptor numbered N or more is open, and N-1 is
           open (it is the lowest-numbered free one that is taken last).
note       Descriptors are allocated lowest first (XSH 2.6), so descriptor
           N-1 being open after the failure shows N-1 could be assigned.
test       t/012-nofile-emfile.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.013
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_FSIZE lowered to 1000 bytes (SIGXFSZ ignored in the
           child where the macro exists), write() of 1000 bytes to an empty
           regular file returns 1000, and a further write() of one byte at
           offset 1000 returns -1 with EFBIG.
note       XSI only for the SIGXFSZ macro used to make the failure
           observable; the failure with EFBIG itself is Base.
test       t/013-fsize-efbig-write.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.014
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_FSIZE lowered to 1000 bytes (SIGXFSZ ignored in the
           child where the macro exists), ftruncate() of a regular file to
           1001 bytes returns -1 with EFBIG and ftruncate() to 1000 bytes
           returns 0.
note       XSI only for the SIGXFSZ macro used to make the failure
           observable; the failure with EFBIG itself is Base.
test       t/014-fsize-efbig-truncate.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.015
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_FSIZE lowered to 1000 bytes and a SIGXFSZ handler
           installed, the write() that would take a file past 1000 bytes
           generates SIGXFSZ (the handler has run by the time the call has
           returned) and returns -1 with EFBIG.
test       t/015-fsize-sigxfsz.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.016
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_CPU lowered to 1 s soft and the default action of
           SIGXCPU in effect, a process that goes on using the processor is
           ended by SIGXCPU (the child gets 5 s of processor time to be
           ended; core files are disabled by RLIMIT_CORE 0).
note       A catching or ignoring process is unspecified, so the default
           disposition is used. An expired wall-clock guard is UNRESOLVED.
timeout    70
test       t/016-cpu-sigxcpu.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.017
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_AS lowered to L (a value the system accepts), an
           mmap() of a file for 2L bytes and a malloc() of 2L bytes both
           fail with ENOMEM (a request larger than the whole limit exceeds
           it).
note       UNRESOLVED when no tried L is accepted by setrlimit().
test       t/017-as-enomem.c

assertion  XSH.getrlimit.018
anchor     functions/getrlimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With RLIMIT_STACK lowered and SIGSEGV at its default action, a
           process that keeps growing its stack (recursion of 4 KiB frames)
           is ended by SIGSEGV before using 256 MiB of stack.
note       UNRESOLVED when no tried limit is accepted by setrlimit() or the
           guard expires. Core files are disabled by RLIMIT_CORE 0.
timeout    70
test       t/018-stack-sigsegv.c
